From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bharat Dintakurti <bdintakurti@nvidia.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 17:12:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] NVIDIA: SAUCE: ACPI: processor_idle: Validate hierarchical
 LPI tables and paths

BugLink: https://bugs.launchpad.net/bugs/2167301

A successfully parsed _LPI hierarchy is not usable for PSCI OSI unless its
hierarchy contract and every firmware state that the coordinated path may
enter are valid.

For callback-based hierarchy extraction, strictly validate revision zero,
the required state fields, DWORD bounds, reserved generic flags, Generic
Register Descriptor framing, counter-register objects, and Enabled Parent
State indices against the complete raw parent table. Keep those checks out
of the legacy flattened parser so existing platform-coordinated firmware
retains its compatibility behavior.

Retain each FFH Generic Address Structure together with its architecture
flags and raw state index. Validate the Arm-defined bit width, bit offset,
access size, and address width. Validate WFI separately without passing its
sentinel to PSCI power-state validation, then validate every other state in
the CPU-local prefix and every exact composite retained for the CPU path.

The LPI architecture flags and the PSCI StateType both describe whether
core context is lost. Reject a direct non-WFI state when they disagree;
the architecture preparation hook performs the same check on the final
post-LevelID composite passed to PSCI.

After a complete path passes validation, widen each retained domain state
latency to the largest accepted per-CPU composite latency observed for that
state. Deferring the update prevents a rejected partial path from
permanently inflating shared-domain policy data.

If validation fails, detach the CPU consumer, release its runtime map,
remove now-unused domains, and reparse the existing flat table. Propagate
cleanup failures instead of hiding retained hierarchy resources behind a
C-state fallback.

